My overall assessment
Strong project opportunity
The strongest aspect of this problem statement is that it combines a meaningful social problem with a practical technical challenge.

It is not simply asking you to build an AI chatbot or another healthcare application. It asks you to address the entire journey from uncertainty to appropriate assistance, even when internet access is unavailable.

Its potential becomes especially compelling when offline functionality, verified resource information, accessibility, and safety-focused AI are treated as essential parts of the solution rather than optional features.

My most important conclusion
I would describe this project as a last-mile healthcare and emergency accessibility platform.

The central issue is that people can have access to a smartphone but still lack timely access to understandable guidance and reliable information about where to get help.

Your solution addresses that gap.

Its real value is not that AI can replace a doctor, diagnose a disease, or dispatch an ambulance. Its value is that it can help a person take a more informed next step when they are uncertain, disconnected, or struggling to find assistance.

A powerful final pitch for the project would be:
"We are building more than an AI healthcare assistant. We are building an offline-first bridge between people in need and the help that already exists around them. Because access to essential assistance should not depend on having a perfect internet connection."

That is the central purpose, social value, and project identity of this idea.